ENFORCEMENT DECREE OF THE FRAMEWORK ACT ON YOUTH

CHAPTER I GENERAL PROVISIONS

Article 1 (Purpose) The purpose of this Decree is to prescribe matters delegated by the Framework Act on Youth and matters necessary for its enforcement.

Article 2 (Youth Day) (1) Youth Day under Article 7 of the Framework Act on Youth (hereinafter referred to as the "Act") shall be the third Saturday of September each year. <Amended on June 20, 2023>
(2) The State and local governments may hold various events to commemorate Youth Day and may support events held to commemorate Youth Day by relevant agencies, corporations, organizations, and others.

CHAPTER II MASTER PLAN FOR YOUTH POLICY AND RELATED MATTERS

Article 3 (Formulation of a master plan for youth policy and related matters) (1) When the Prime Minister intends to formulate or amend the master plan for youth policy under Article 8(1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "master plan"), the Prime Minister may request the heads of relevant central administrative agencies and the Special Metropolitan City Mayor, Metropolitan City Mayors, the Special Self-Governing City Mayor, Do Governors, and the Special Self-Governing Province Governor (hereinafter referred to as "Mayors/Do Governors") to submit necessary materials. In such cases, the heads of central administrative agencies and Mayors/Do Governors receiving the request shall cooperate unless there are special grounds for not doing so.
(2) "Matters prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 8(2)7 of the Act means the following matters:
1. Changes in the economic and social environment related to youth policy;
2. Expansion of youth participation in procedures for formulating youth policy;
3. Promotion of communication between youths and the State and local governments;
4. Balanced development of youth policy among local governments;
5. Sharing of information and publicity concerning youth policy.
(3) When the formulation or amendment of the master plan has been finalized pursuant to Article 8(4) of the Act, the Prime Minister shall notify the heads of relevant central administrative agencies and Mayors/Do Governors without delay.

Article 4 (Formulation of annual implementation plans and related matters) (1) The Prime Minister shall establish guidelines necessary for formulating the annual implementation plans under Article 9(1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "implementation plans") and notify the heads of relevant central administrative agencies and Mayors/Do Governors of those guidelines by November 30 each year.
(2) The heads of relevant central administrative agencies and Mayors/Do Governors notified of the guidelines under paragraph (1) shall formulate their implementation plans and submit them to the Prime Minister by January 31 of the following year.
(3) Where necessary to examine the implementation plans submitted under paragraph (2), the Prime Minister may hear the opinions of experts in the relevant fields.

Article 5 (Analysis and assessment of implementation results and related matters) (1) The Prime Minister shall establish guidelines for preparing the results achieved under implementation plans and notify the heads of relevant central administrative agencies and Mayors/Do Governors of those guidelines by December 31 each year.
(2) The heads of relevant central administrative agencies and Mayors/Do Governors notified of the guidelines under paragraph (1) shall prepare the results achieved under the previous year's implementation plans and submit them to the Prime Minister by the last day of February of the following year.
(3) By June 30 each year, the Prime Minister shall notify the heads of relevant central administrative agencies and Mayors/Do Governors of the results of the comprehensive analysis and assessment of the implementation results submitted under paragraph (2) by the relevant central administrative agencies and the Special Metropolitan City, Metropolitan Cities, the Special Self-Governing City, Do, and the Special Self-Governing Province (hereinafter referred to as "Cities/Do").

Article 6 (Designation of youth policy analysis and assessment support institutes) (1) A research institute seeking designation as an institute supporting the analysis and assessment of youth policy under Article 9(4) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as an "analysis and assessment support institute") shall submit to the Prime Minister an application for designation as a youth policy analysis and assessment support institute in attached Form 1, together with the following documents (including submission by electronic document):
1. A business plan;
2. Details of personnel and facilities related to youth policy analysis and assessment;
3. A record of youth policy analysis and assessment performed (only where such a record exists).
(2) The Prime Minister may designate a research institute that has applied for designation under paragraph (1) as an analysis and assessment support institute where the Prime Minister considers that it has sufficient personnel, facilities, and expertise to analyze and assess youth policy. In such cases, when the Prime Minister designates an analysis and assessment support institute, the Prime Minister shall issue a certificate of designation as a youth policy analysis and assessment support institute in attached Form 2.
(3) When the Prime Minister designates an analysis and assessment support institute under paragraph (2), the Prime Minister shall post that fact on an Internet website or by other such means.

Article 7 (Revocation of designation of analysis and assessment support institutes) (1) Where an analysis and assessment support institute falls under any of the following subparagraphs, the Prime Minister may revoke its designation pursuant to Article 9(5) of the Act; provided, the Prime Minister shall revoke its designation where subparagraph 1, 3, or 4 applies:
1. Where it obtained designation by fraud or other improper means;
2. Where it no longer satisfies the personnel and facility requirements that applied when it sought designation under Article 6(1);
3. Where it is considered unable to perform its duties properly because it has performed analysis and assessment inadequately through intent or gross negligence;
4. Where the analysis and assessment support institute requests revocation of its designation, stating justifiable grounds.
(2) The Prime Minister shall hold a hearing before revoking the designation of an analysis and assessment support institute under paragraph (1).

Article 8 (Survey of the actual conditions of youths) (1) A survey of actual conditions under Article 11(1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a "survey of actual conditions") shall include the following matters concerning youths:
1. General characteristics of youths, such as gender, age, academic background, and marital status;
2. Economic conditions, such as income and expenditure;
3. Living environments, such as housing and consumer life;
4. Jobs, such as employment status and working environments;
5. Competency development, such as education and vocational training;
6. Cultural environments, such as recreation and leisure;
7. Health and welfare, such as health and welfare services;
8. Other matters necessary for formulating effective youth policy.
(2) The heads of relevant central administrative agencies shall conduct a survey of actual conditions in each field under their jurisdiction every 2 years; provided, they may conduct additional surveys as needed where further investigation is necessary due to changes in the social environment or other circumstances.

Article 9 (Entrustment of youth policy research projects) To efficiently carry out research projects for formulating, implementing, and supporting youth policy under Article 12(1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "youth policy research projects"), the Prime Minister may entrust youth policy research projects to any of the following specialized institutions or organizations pursuant to paragraph (2) of that Article:
1. A government-funded research institute established under the Act on the Establishment, Operation and Fostering of Government-Funded Research Institutions that conducts research on youth policy;
2. A university under subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Higher Education Act that has a department or major related to youth policy (including its affiliated research institutes);
3. A nonprofit corporation established under Article 32 of the Civil Act that conducts research or performs work related to youth policy.

CHAPTER III OVERALL MANAGEMENT AND COORDINATION OF YOUTH POLICY

Article 10 (Matters for deliberation and coordination by the Youth Policy Coordination Committee) "Matters prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 13(2)7 of the Act means the following matters:
1. The scope of committees under Article 20(1);
2. The scope of committees falling under Article 20(2)4(a) and the ratio of youths commissioned to those committees.
[This Article wholly amended on September 12, 2023]

Article 11 (Composition of the Youth Policy Coordination Committee) (1) The chairperson of the Youth Policy Coordination Committee under Article 13(1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "Coordination Committee") (hereinafter referred to as the "Chairperson") shall represent the Coordination Committee and exercise overall control over its affairs.
(2) The vice-chairpersons of the Coordination Committee shall be persons designated by the Chairperson, one from among the members under Article 13(4)1 of the Act and one from among the members under Article 13(4)3 of the Act.
(3) "Heads of central administrative agencies prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 13(4)1 of the Act means the Minister of National Defense and the Minister of Planning and Budget. <Amended on December 30, 2025>
(4) The Coordination Committee shall have 1 executive secretary to handle its affairs, and the executive secretary shall be the public official at office-head level responsible for youth policy in the Office for Government Policy Coordination. <Amended on February 15, 2022>

Article 12 (Operation of the Coordination Committee) (1) The Chairperson shall convene meetings of the Coordination Committee and preside over them.
(2) The vice-chairpersons of the Coordination Committee shall assist the Chairperson, and where the Chairperson is unable to perform the duties of the office due to unavoidable circumstances, the vice-chairperson designated by the Chairperson shall act on the Chairperson's behalf.
(3) A meeting of the Coordination Committee shall open with a majority of its incumbent members present, and resolutions shall require the affirmative votes of a majority of the members present.
(4) Where necessary for the performance of the Coordination Committee's duties, the Chairperson may have the heads of relevant central administrative agencies or Mayors/Do Governors attend a meeting of the Coordination Committee and present their opinions on agenda items relating to fields under their jurisdiction, or may have relevant experts attend a meeting and hear their opinions.

Article 13 (Removal of members of the Coordination Committee and related matters) (1) A consultative council under Article 182(1)1 or 3 of the Local Autonomy Act may withdraw its recommendation of a member under Article 13(4)2 of the Act where that member falls under any of the following subparagraphs: <Amended on December 16, 2021>
1. Where the member becomes unable to perform the duties of the office due to a physical or mental disability;
2. Where the member has engaged in misconduct related to the duties of the office;
3. Where the member is considered unsuitable to serve as a member due to neglect of duties, impairment of dignity, or other grounds;
4. Where the member personally expresses the view that it is difficult to perform the duties of the office.
(2) Where a member under Article 13(4)3 of the Act falls under any of the subparagraphs of paragraph (1), the Chairperson may remove that member from the commission.

Article 14 (Composition of the working committee) (1) The working committee under Article 13(7) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "working committee") shall consist of no more than 20 members, including 1 chairperson and 2 vice-chairpersons.
(2) The Minister for Government Policy Coordination shall be the chairperson of the working committee, and its members shall be the following persons: <Amended on December 30, 2025>
1. The Vice Minister of Finance and Economy, the Vice Minister of Education, the Vice Minister of Health and Welfare, the Vice Minister of Employment and Labor, the Vice Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, the Vice Minister of SMEs and Startups, and the Vice Minister for Government Policy Coordination. In such cases, where an agency has more than one vice minister or deputy head, the member shall be the vice minister or deputy head designated by the head of that agency;
2. A deputy head of the local government to which a member under Article 13(4)2 of the Act belongs, designated by the head of that local government;
3. Persons commissioned by the chairperson of the working committee from among persons with extensive expertise and experience in youth policy or persons representing youths, such as representatives of youth organizations.
(3) The vice-chairpersons of the working committee shall be persons designated by its chairperson, one from among the members under paragraph (2)1 and one from among the members under paragraph (2)3.
(4) The term of office of a member commissioned under paragraph (2)3 shall be 2 years; provided, the term of a member commissioned in the capacity of a representative of an agency or organization shall be the period during which that person holds the representative position.
(5) The working committee shall have 1 executive secretary to handle its affairs, and the executive secretary shall be a person designated by the Minister for Government Policy Coordination from among public officials belonging to the Senior Executive Service in the Office for Government Policy Coordination.

Article 15 (Operation of the working committee) (1) The chairperson of the working committee shall convene meetings and preside over them.
(2) The vice-chairpersons of the working committee shall assist its chairperson, and where the chairperson is unable to perform the duties of the office due to unavoidable circumstances, the vice-chairperson of the working committee designated by its chairperson shall act on the chairperson's behalf.
(3) The chairperson of the working committee shall report matters deliberated and resolved upon by the working committee to the Coordination Committee.
(4) Where necessary for the performance of the working committee's duties, its chairperson may have vice ministers or public officials belonging to the Senior Executive Service in relevant central administrative agencies, or deputy heads or public officials belonging to the Senior Executive Service in Cities/Do, attend the working committee and present their opinions on agenda items relating to fields under their jurisdiction, or may have members of an expert committee under Article 16(1) or relevant experts attend a meeting and hear their opinions.

Article 16 (Expert committees) (1) Where necessary, the chairperson of the working committee may establish within the working committee an expert committee under Article 13(7) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as an "expert committee") for each of the following fields:
1. Jobs;
2. Education;
3. Housing;
4. Daily life;
5. Participation and rights;
6. Other fields considered necessary by the chairperson of the working committee.
(2) An expert committee shall consist of no more than 10 members, including 1 chairperson.
(3) Members of an expert committee shall be commissioned by the chairperson of the working committee from among persons with extensive expertise and experience in youth policy or persons representing youths, such as representatives of youth organizations, and the chairperson of the expert committee shall be designated by the chairperson of the working committee from among the members of that expert committee.

Article 17 (Allowances and other expenses) Relevant experts attending meetings of the Coordination Committee or working committee under Article 12(4) or 15(4) may be paid allowances, travel expenses, and other necessary expenses within the limits of the budget.

Article 18 (Organization and operation of the secretariat and related matters) (1) The secretariat under Article 13(8) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "Secretariat") shall perform the following duties:
1. Support for the operation of the Coordination Committee, working committee, and expert committees;
2. Support for preparing and reviewing agenda items for deliberation by the Coordination Committee and working committee, and for consultation and coordination concerning those agenda items;
3. Specialized investigation and research relating to matters for deliberation and coordination by the Coordination Committee;
4. Publicity for the activities of the Coordination Committee and external cooperation;
5. Other duties considered necessary by the Chairperson to support the performance of the Coordination Committee's duties.
(2) The Secretariat shall have 1 head, and the public official at office-head level responsible for youth policy in the Office for Government Policy Coordination shall concurrently serve as its head. <Amended on February 15, 2022>
(3) Where necessary for the performance of the Secretariat's duties, the Minister for Government Policy Coordination may request the heads of relevant administrative agencies, research institutes, or organizations to dispatch public officials or employees under their jurisdiction or allow them to serve concurrently.

Article 19 (Detailed operating rules) Except as provided in this Decree, matters necessary for the operation of the Coordination Committee and the composition, operation, and other affairs of the working committee and expert committees shall be determined by the Chairperson following a resolution of the Coordination Committee.

Article 20 (Scope of committees to which youths are to be commissioned and related matters) (1) "Committees prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the former part of Article 15(2) of the Act means committees falling under any of the following subparagraphs that are determined, respectively, by the Prime Minister or a Mayor/Do Governor following deliberation and coordination by the Coordination Committee or the local youth policy coordination committee under Article 14(1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a "local youth policy coordination committee"): <Amended on September 12, 2023>
1. Committees dealing with individual cases;
2. Committees related to foreign affairs, national defense, or security policy;
3. Committees for handling internal affairs of administrative agencies, such as personnel management, audits, or contracts;
4. Other committees for which mandatory commissioning of youths is considered difficult in light of their duties, nature, composition, or other circumstances.
(2) The scope of committees in which youths must constitute at least 3/10 of the commissioned members under Article 15(2) of the Act shall be as follows: <Newly inserted September 12, 2023>
1. The Coordination Committee;
2. Local youth policy coordination committees;
3. The Special Committee on Youth Employment Promotion under Article 4(1) of the Special Act on the Promotion of Youth Employment;
4. Other committees falling under either of the following lettered items:
(a) Committees constituted by the heads of relevant central administrative agencies and determined by the Prime Minister following deliberation and coordination by the Coordination Committee;
(b) Committees constituted by Mayors/Do Governors and determined by the relevant Mayor/Do Governor following deliberation and coordination by the local youth policy coordination committee.
(3) When a committee is constituted under Article 15(2) of the Act, the ratio of youths commissioned to each committee shall be as follows: <Newly inserted September 12, 2023; April 14, 2026>
1. Committees under paragraph (2)1 through 3: At least 3/10;
2. Committees under paragraph (2)4: A ratio of at least 3/10 determined, respectively, by the Prime Minister or a Mayor/Do Governor following deliberation and coordination by the Coordination Committee or Local Youth Policy Committee;
3. Other committees: At least 2/10.
(4) When the Prime Minister determines or changes the scope of committees falling under paragraph (1) or (2)4(a), or the ratio of commissioned youths under paragraph (3)2, the Prime Minister shall notify the heads of relevant central administrative agencies and publicly announce that fact in the Official Gazette and on an Internet website or by other such means. <Amended on September 12, 2023>

Article 20-2 (Qualifications of young talent and related matters) "Young talent with certain qualifications prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 15-2(1) of the Act means youths who meet the criteria determined by the Prime Minister in consideration of their fields of activity, fields of interest, and other matters, and who have personally applied to the head of a relevant central administrative agency or a Mayor/Do Governor to participate in the youth policy decision-making process, or have been recommended by the head of any of the following institutions or organizations:
1. A relevant central administrative agency or local government;
2. A public institution (meaning a public institution under Article 4 of the Act on the Management of Public Institutions; hereinafter the same shall apply);
3. A nonprofit nongovernmental organization under the Assistance for Non-Profit, Non-Governmental Organizations Act;
4. A university, research institute, or other comparable institution.
[This Article newly inserted on February 15, 2022]

Article 20-3 (Scope and procedures for collecting information on young talent) (1) The heads of relevant central administrative agencies and Mayors/Do Governors may collect the following information on young talent (hereinafter referred to as "young talent information") pursuant to Article 15-2(1) of the Act:
1. Name, gender, age, and contact details;
2. Academic background, career history, qualifications, and honors and awards;
3. Current and former positions;
4. Fields of expertise or interest;
5. Publication history of major books, papers, and other works.
(2) Where the head of a relevant central administrative agency or a Mayor/Do Governor intends to collect young talent information from the person concerned, the head or Mayor/Do Governor shall have that person prepare the information in writing and send it to the head of the relevant central administrative agency or the Mayor/Do Governor, or shall enable that person to access the young talent database under Article 20-4 and enter the information.
(3) When collecting young talent information under paragraph (2), the heads of relevant central administrative agencies and Mayors/Do Governors shall inform the persons concerned in advance that the collected information may be provided for the purpose of promoting youth participation in the policy decision-making processes of State agencies, local governments, or public institutions.
[This Article newly inserted on February 15, 2022]

Article 20-4 (Establishment and operation of the young talent database and related matters) (1) The Government may establish and operate a database on young talent (hereinafter referred to as the "young talent database") to enable effective management and shared use of the young talent information collected by the heads of relevant central administrative agencies or Mayors/Do Governors pursuant to Article 15-2 of the Act and Article 20-3 of this Decree.
(2) The Prime Minister shall determine matters necessary for establishing and operating the young talent database, including the methods and procedures for registering in that database the young talent information collected by the heads of relevant central administrative agencies and Mayors/Do Governors, and the shared use of young talent information.
[This Article newly inserted on February 15, 2022]

Article 20-5 (Protection of young talent information and related matters) (1) A person whose information is registered in the young talent database may, by mail, fax, email, or other such means, request the head of a relevant central administrative agency or a Mayor/Do Governor to permit inspection of that information.
(2) The head of a relevant central administrative agency or a Mayor/Do Governor who receives a request under paragraph (1) shall make the information available for inspection without delay.
(3) A person whose information is registered in the young talent database may, by mail, fax, email, or other such means, request the head of a relevant central administrative agency or a Mayor/Do Governor to correct or destroy that information.
(4) The head of a relevant central administrative agency or a Mayor/Do Governor who receives a request under paragraph (3) shall correct or destroy the information within 7 days and inform the person concerned of the result.
(5) Where an institution that has been provided with young talent information or has directly inspected it pursuant to Article 19-3(4) of the State Public Officials Act, as applied mutatis mutandis under Article 15-2(2) of the Act, engages in conduct such as using the information for purposes other than the intended purpose, the heads of relevant central administrative agencies and Mayors/Do Governors may restrict the provision of information to, or direct inspection by, that institution.
[This Article newly inserted on February 15, 2022]

Article 21 (Designation of youth policy officers and related matters) (1) Pursuant to Article 16(1) of the Act, the heads of relevant central administrative agencies and Mayors/Do Governors shall designate as youth policy officers persons who are public officials belonging to the Senior Executive Service of the relevant agency, or public officials of an equivalent grade, and who hold positions with overall responsibility for youth policy affairs. Where necessary for the performance of the youth policy officers' duties, they may designate Grade 5 public officials or public officials of an equivalent grade as supporting personnel. <Amended on September 6, 2022>
(2) A youth policy officer shall perform the following duties; provided, the duty under subparagraph 3 shall apply only to youth policy officers designated by Mayors/Do Governors:
1. Overall coordination of and support for the relevant agency's youth policy;
2. Consolidation and coordination of the relevant agency's implementation plans and examination of implementation results;
3. Operation of the relevant agency's local youth policy coordination committee;
4. Education and publicity concerning the relevant agency's youth policy;
5. Linkage and cooperation between youth policy and the relevant agency's other policies, plans, and related matters;
6. Cooperation among agencies on youth policy affairs;
7. Other duties relating to the relevant agency's youth policy.

Article 21-2 (Recruitment of specialized personnel for youth policy) (1) Where necessary for the specialized performance of the following duties, the heads of relevant central administrative agencies may recruit the specialized personnel for youth policy under Article 16(1) of the Act as specialized public officials with a fixed term of office, within the limits of the budget: <Amended on June 20, 2023>
1. Identifying youths' perceptions and attitudes concerning the policies of the relevant central administrative agency;
2. Gathering youths' opinions on, and promoting their participation in, the implementation of the relevant central administrative agency's policies;
3. Reviewing matters instructed by the head of the relevant central administrative agency in relation to youth policy;
4. Supporting the operation of policy advisory bodies relating to youths established in the relevant central administrative agency;
5. Other duties comparable to those under subparagraphs 1 through 4 that the head of the relevant central administrative agency considers necessary for youth participation in policy.
(2) The specialized public officials with a fixed term of office under paragraph (1) shall be recruited from among youths. <Amended on June 20, 2023>
[This Article newly inserted on September 6, 2022]
[Title amended on June 20, 2023]

CHAPTER III-2 ESTABLISHMENT OF THE FOUNDATION FOR IMPLEMENTING YOUTH POLICY <Newly inserted September 12, 2023>

Article 21-3 (Establishment and operation of youth facilities and related matters) (1) When the State or a local government establishes and operates a youth facility pursuant to Article 24-2(1) of the Act, it shall have specialized personnel to support youth development.
(2) Where the State or a local government considers it necessary for the efficient establishment and operation of youth facilities pursuant to Article 24-2(1) of the Act, it may request necessary cooperation from relevant agencies, corporations, organizations, and others.
(3) Where the State or a local government entrusts the establishment and operation of a youth facility pursuant to Article 24-2(2) of the Act, it shall post information concerning the entrusted person and the details of the entrustment on an Internet website.
[This Article newly inserted on September 12, 2023]

Article 21-4 (Scope of support for youth organizations and others) The scope of administrative and financial support by the State under Article 24-3(2) of the Act shall be as follows:
1. Support for project costs necessary for the following youth support activities:
(a) Projects concerning youth development, including the promotion of youth employment, support for business start-ups, support for competency development, housing support, promotion of welfare, support for financial life, and support for cultural activities;
(b) International events relating to youths in Korea and abroad for the promotion of international peace and international cooperation;
(c) Other projects that the head of a central administrative agency or a local government considers necessary to develop or revitalize youth organizations and others;
2. Support for counseling, publicity, and education concerning youth support activities;
3. Other matters considered necessary by the Prime Minister for the efficient performance of youth support activities.
[This Article newly inserted on September 12, 2023]

Article 21-5 (Criteria for designating youth support centers and related matters) (1) The criteria for designating regional youth support centers under the part of Article 24-4(1) of the Act other than its subparagraphs (hereinafter referred to as "regional centers") shall be as follows:
1. There shall be at least 1 office, 1 counseling room, and 1 classroom necessary for the performance of duties;
2. There shall be a separate space that may be used as a place for interaction among youths;
3. There shall be a person with overall responsibility for the duties and a separate department to perform the duties;
4. Personnel shall have completed a youth support training course designated by the Prime Minister.
(2) The criteria for designating the central youth support center under Article 24-4(2) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "central center") shall be as follows:
1. There shall be at least 1 office, 1 counseling room, and 1 classroom necessary for the performance of duties;
2. There shall be a separate space that may be used as a place for interaction among youths;
3. There shall be a person with overall responsibility for the duties and at least 10 full-time employees to perform the duties;
4. There shall be at least 3 separate departments to perform the duties;
5. There shall be a record, during the most recent 3 years, of performing projects concerning youth support, youth development, or the establishment of collaborative systems relating to youth policy.
(3) A person seeking designation as a regional center or the central center shall submit an application for designation to the Prime Minister, together with the documents specified in the following categories:
1. Regional center: The following documents:
(a) Documents proving compliance with the designation criteria under paragraph (1);
(b) A project implementation plan;
(c) A financial management plan and documents proving the financial condition;
2. Central center: The following documents:
(a) Documents proving compliance with the designation criteria under paragraph (2);
(b) A project implementation plan;
(c) A financial management plan and documents proving the financial condition;
(d) Documents concerning the internal operating rules of the relevant institution or organization.
(4) Where necessary for the efficient designation of regional centers or the central center, the Prime Minister may request relevant materials from the heads of local governments. In such cases, the heads of local governments shall comply unless there are special grounds for not doing so.
(5) When the Prime Minister designates a regional center or the central center, the Prime Minister shall post the details on an Internet website.
(6) The designation period for regional centers and the central center shall be 3 years each.
(7) Except as provided in paragraphs (1) through (6), the Prime Minister shall determine detailed matters necessary for the designation of support centers or the central center.
[This Article newly inserted on September 12, 2023]

Article 21-6 (Criteria for revocation of designation) The criteria for revoking the designation of a regional center or the central center under Article 24-4(4) of the Act shall be as set out in the Annex.
[This Article newly inserted on September 12, 2023]

Article 21-7 (Designation of an institution dedicated to operating the integrated information system) (1) The criteria for designating an institution dedicated to operating the integrated information system under Article 24-5(3) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "dedicated operating institution") shall be as follows:
1. The duties of the relevant institution, organization, or other entity shall relate to youth policy;
2. There shall be at least 2 specialized personnel in the field of informatization necessary for the performance of duties;
3. There shall be an office necessary for the performance of duties;
4. There shall be a separate dedicated organization to perform the duties;
5. There shall be space for the storage, management, and operation of information equipment and information systems;
6. There shall be standards or rules for handling duties, information security, and personal information protection.
(2) When the Prime Minister designates a dedicated operating institution pursuant to Article 24-5(3) of the Act, the Prime Minister shall post the details on an Internet website.
(3) Except as provided in paragraphs (1) and (2), the Prime Minister shall determine detailed matters necessary for the designation of dedicated operating institutions.
[This Article newly inserted on September 12, 2023]

Article 21-8 (Designation of youth-friendly cities) (1) The criteria for designating youth-friendly cities under Article 24-6(1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "youth-friendly cities") shall be as follows:
1. There shall be a foundation for developing a youth-friendly city;
2. There shall be an organizational structure and personnel for developing a youth-friendly city;
3. There shall be a record of work relating to youth participation, youth development, enhancement of youths' capabilities, improvement of their quality of life, and other such matters;
4. Other criteria considered necessary by the Prime Minister for designating youth-friendly cities shall be met.
(2) A Special Self-Governing City Mayor, Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or head of a Si/Gun/Gu (meaning the head of an autonomous Gu) seeking designation as a youth-friendly city pursuant to Article 24-6(2) of the Act shall submit the following documents to the Prime Minister:
1. Documents proving compliance with the designation criteria under paragraph (1);
2. Documents concerning a plan to create a youth-friendly city that includes the following matters:
(a) Policy measures for youth development and enhancement of youths' capabilities in the relevant local government;
(b) Policy measures for improving the quality of life of youths in the relevant local government;
(c) Measures for promoting the rights and interests of youths in the relevant local government.
(3) When the Prime Minister designates a youth-friendly city pursuant to Article 24-6(2) of the Act, the Prime Minister shall post the details on an Internet website.
(4) The designation period for a youth-friendly city shall be 5 years.
(5) Except as provided in paragraphs (1) through (4), the Prime Minister shall determine detailed matters necessary for the designation of youth-friendly cities.
[This Article newly inserted on September 12, 2023]

CHAPTER IV SUPPLEMENTARY PROVISIONS

Article 22 Deleted <September 12, 2023>

Article 23 (Awards) (1) When conferring awards pursuant to Article 26 of the Act, the heads of relevant central administrative agencies may receive recommendations of candidates from institutions or organizations relating to youths.
(2) Except as provided in paragraph (1), the heads of relevant central administrative agencies shall determine matters necessary for conferring awards.

ADDENDA <Presidential Decree No. 30901, August 4, 2020>

Article 1 (Enforcement date) This Decree shall enter into force on August 5, 2020.

Article 2 (Special cases concerning formulation of the master plan) The first master plan formulated pursuant to Article 3 after this Decree enters into force shall be formulated by December 31, 2020.

Article 3 (Special cases concerning notification of guidelines for formulating implementation plans) Notwithstanding Article 4(1), the guidelines necessary for formulating implementation plans for 2021 shall be notified to the heads of relevant central administrative agencies and Mayors/Do Governors by December 31, 2020.

Article 4 (Special cases concerning the scope of committees to which youths are to be commissioned and the commissioning ratios) Where a committee that is required under Article 20(1) to commission youths as at least a certain proportion of its commissioned members does not meet the youth commissioning ratio under the respective subparagraphs of that paragraph, its commissioned members shall be appointed from among youths until the youth commissioning ratio under the relevant subparagraph is met.

ADDENDA (Enforcement Decree of the Local Autonomy Act) <Presidential Decree No. 32223, December 16, 2021>

